    "tree_id": "b2f0c795b31a985e9f51cd47d8bd793605117879",
    "message": "Have the design name the modules, what each hides and the decisions they share\n\nStep 2 of the design brief asked for \"what the chosen option changes in\nthe code\", and sessions answered by deriving its consequences down to\nthe implementation. On benetics-ag/benetics#22434 the design prescribed\nan override of a private docling method, a test through a private\nfunction and the CI steps to run it, and the implementer and the\nstandards review then deferred to the design as if it were the ticket\n(benetics-ag/benetics#22665).\n\nStep 2 now asks for a design in the glossary's sense. It names the\nmodules the change touches, what each is responsible for and its\ninterface, everything the other modules must know of it, and leaves\neverything else to the implementer: Ousterhout's module and interface,\nParnas's information hiding. The decisions more than one module depends\non, the seam where the option attaches first of all, are chosen as the\nsoftware-design skill chooses between options, so the alternatives and\nthe case against each are recorded where the implementer reads them.\nThe template's Decisions section goes: the choice sentence does the\nrecording, in whatever form the session gives it.\n\nEvery sentence was ablated on its own, on #22434 and on three of\nLinewright's own design runs (#511, #549, #674), one run per cell with\nstep 1 held fixed. Removing the hiding sentence brought back function\nbodies in prose on three of four cases; removing the choice sentence\nlost the recorded alternatives on four of four. The rest changed nothing\nwhen removed and are gone: \"where it lives\", a completion criterion, an\nescape hatch for a library without a seam, the library clause, the lift\nof the skill's strike rule, \"an interface is one both sides support\" and\nthe opening sentence. \"A method or function\" stays as Ousterhout has it;\neight runs with it produced no function-sized module.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
